<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suva</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suva is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, on the south-east coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viti-levu">Viti Levu</a>. It replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levuka">Levuka</a> as the capital of the British colony in 1882 and remained the capital at independence in 1970. It is the main port and commercial centre of Fiji and hosts the University of the South Pacific and many regional organisations.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suva.jpg" alt="Suva" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maksym Kozlenko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suva,_Fiji_77.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Suva lies around a harbour on a hilly peninsula in the south-east corner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viti-levu">Viti Levu</a>, between Laucala Bay and Suva Harbour, in Rewa Province. The mountains to the north and west catch the south-east trade winds, so conditions are moist all year. The city has a tropical rainforest climate and averages about 3,000 millimetres of rain a year, with no true dry season. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>'s second governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-hamilton-gordon-1st-baron-stanmore">Sir Arthur Gordon</a>, is said to have remarked that he had never seen it rain anywhere as it rains in Suva. Although the city is almost surrounded by sea, its coast is lined with mangroves, and a significant part of the centre, including the Parliament buildings, stands on reclaimed mangrove swamp.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1868, when Suva was still a small village, the Bauan chieftain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seru-epenisa-cakobau">Seru Epenisa Cakobau</a> granted 5,000 square kilometres of land to the Australian-based Polynesia Company in exchange for its promise to pay off debts owed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. More than a tenth of that land, 575 square kilometres, was near Suva. The company meant to develop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> farming, but the land and climate proved unsuitable. Control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji Islands</a> was ceded to the United Kingdom in 1874.</p><p>The colonial authorities moved the capital to Suva from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levuka">Levuka</a>, on Ovalau, because Levuka's position between a steep mountain and the sea made expansion impractical. Colonel F. E. Pratt of the Royal Engineers, appointed Surveyor-General in 1875, designed the new capital, assisted by W. Stephens and Colonel R. W. Stewart. Suva became a municipality in 1910 under the Municipal Constitution Ordinance of 1909, and its area stayed at one square mile until 1952, when it annexed the Muanikau and Samabula wards. In October of that year it was designated a city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>'s first. Later it took in Tamavua and the Cunningham area.</p><p>Among its colonial-period landmarks are the Suva City Carnegie Library, built in 1909, and Government House, built in 1882, destroyed by a lightning fire in 1921 and rebuilt in 1928. The government building complex stands on a drained creek bed, with foundations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reinforced-concrete">reinforced concrete</a> pilings driven in from 1935, and was completed in 1939. The Fiji Museum was founded in 1904 and moved to Thurston Gardens in 1954.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Suva is the capital and most populous settlement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, with 93,874 people at the 2017 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> and a metropolitan population of 268,423. Indigenous Fijians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-fijians">Indo-Fijians</a> form the bulk of its population, alongside Rotumans, Lauans, Europeans, part-Europeans and Chinese. English is the most widely spoken language, with Fijian, Fiji Hindi and others also used. It is the site of the main campus of the University of the South Pacific, and Kings Wharf handles much of Fiji's international shipping. Suva hosted the Pacific Games in 1963, 1979 and 2003.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Suva” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Fiji Museum holds Uluilakeba's manuscript, but by the museum's description he composed the song in 1918, while he was training as a civil servant in <b>Suva</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/isa-lei">Isa Lei</a></li><li><blockquote>The first governor, Sir Arthur Gordon, laid down the colony's lasting system, and the capital moved from Levuka to <b>Suva</b> in 1882.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-fiji">Colony of Fiji</a></li></ul></section>
